Protocol Target Lab third-party source notes — protocol workers

The project's original Rust manager, Web application, HTTP/DNS/MQTT/Telnet/IEC104/MQTT-SN/RTP/RTCP/DL-T645/BFD wrappers and test clients,
and integration scripts are distributed under the accompanying MIT LICENSE.
The IEC101 native C wrapper is instead GPL-3.0-or-later, as identified in its
source; its Python supervisor/client and local serial helper use MIT.
Third-party components retain their original licenses. Full license and copyright
texts for the locked Linux Rust dependency closure and bundled Web components
are in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt. The version/source/file index is
third-party/licenses.json (third-party-licenses.json in the installed docs).
The deb installs both files under /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab. Keep LICENSE,
this notice, the full license bundle and its index with standalone binaries.

The bundle includes nested upstream notices and the complete Vite notice file.
The include_dir crates omit their license text from their published archives;
third-party/upstream/include_dir preserves the license from the exact upstream
commit recorded in those archives. Generation and verification require only
prepared Cargo/npm caches; scripts/collect-third-party-licenses.py never downloads.

Rust dependencies:
  Cargo.toml lists direct dependencies. Cargo.lock records exact resolved package
  versions and registry checksums. Crate license declarations and upstream source
  metadata are available through `cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1`.
  Dependency source archives and LICENSE/NOTICE files must accompany any future
  complete offline source distribution; a Cargo lockfile alone is not that bundle.

Web dependencies:
  frontend/package.json lists direct dependencies. frontend/package-lock.json
  records package versions, source locations, integrity values and supplied license
  metadata. Preserve upstream license files when distributing dependency materials.

HTTP runtime image:
  Python 3.12, Python Software Foundation License, https://www.python.org/psf/license/
  Official Python image build sources: https://github.com/docker-library/python
  Debian base packages retain their package licenses in /usr/share/doc inside the
  image. images/http/base-image.lock identifies the selected public base digest.
  The worker uses only the Python standard library; no pip packages are installed.

Docker Engine and CLI:
  The manager invokes the system Docker CLI with structured arguments. The engine
  and its CLI are provided by the Ubuntu docker.io system package, not copied
  into this source repository or the manager deb. Upstream: https://github.com/moby/moby and
  https://github.com/docker/cli. Docker installation/dependency preparation is an
  administrator or offline-bundle preparation step, never a package-maintainer
  script download.

DNS runtime image:
  dnsmasq 2.90, copyright Simon Kelley and upstream contributors, is offered under
  GPL version 2 or (at your option) version 3. Complete corresponding unmodified
  source, both license texts, exact build recipes/flags and source SHA-256 are in
  third-party/dnsmasq and images/dns. The DNS image includes these materials at
  /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab-dns; scripts/dns-assets.sh exports them beside
  the saved runtime image. Keep them with redistributed DNS images. The MIT
  license of the original Python wrapper/client does not relicense dnsmasq.
  Python/Debian base components retain their own licenses as described above.
  dnsmasq is in the separately imported image, not the native manager deb.

MQTT runtime image:
  Eclipse Mosquitto 2.0.18 is distributed under the upstream choice of Eclipse
  Public License 2.0 or Eclipse Distribution License 1.0. Complete original
  source, documented upstream packet-size patches, both licenses and bundled
  uthash/utlist notices are preserved in
  third-party/mosquitto and in the runtime image. images/mqtt contains the exact
  build recipes and dependency/source lock. scripts/mqtt-assets.sh exports these
  materials beside the saved image. Mosquitto is not copied into the manager deb;
  original Python wrappers/clients use MIT and base components keep their licenses.

Telnet runtime image:
  telnetlib3 4.0.3 is ISC licensed; wcwidth 0.6.0 is MIT licensed. telnetlib3 also
  bundles a CPython telnetlib backport. Complete original CPython/Python license
  and provenance materials, upstream package source/wheels, package licenses and
  dependency hashes are preserved under third-party/telnetlib3 and in the image.
  images/telnet and scripts/telnet-assets.sh provide offline runtime build/export
  materials. These Python components run in the separate image, not in the native
  manager binary; the Rust/Web license bundle index describes its own closure.

IEC104 runtime image:
  c104 2.2.1 and its native lib60870 implementation are GPLv3 software. A documented
  project patch preserves incremental state when a TCP length byte has not yet
  arrived. It is not represented as an upstream backport. The full
  public source distribution includes native sources and bundled mbedtls/pybind11
  materials with their original notices. Pinned sources, wheel, build recipes
  and separate dependency provenance are in third-party/iec104 and images/iec104,
  and accompany the runtime/source export. These components run in the separate
  worker image, not in the native Rust/Web manager. Preserve upstream licenses
  and complete source/build materials when redistributing the image.

MQTT-SN runtime image:
  Eclipse Paho MQTT-SN Gateway 1.5.1 is built from the pinned public embedded-C
  source with its original Eclipse license/notice texts retained. A minimal
  project capacity guard returns native congestion rather than dereferencing
  a missing topic entry when the registration table is full. The patch is not
  represented as an upstream backport. Source and
  dependency provenance are in third-party/paho-mqtt-sn and images/mqtt-sn.
  The same supervised image includes the documented Mosquitto 2.0.18 source,
  full-packet backports and licenses described above for its private broker.
  Public build/source materials accompany the separately imported image; the
  Rust/Web manager dependency index is a separate closure.

RTP runtime image:
  The receiver, private read-only observer and client are original MIT-licensed
  Python standard-library code. No third-party media stack is bundled. The pinned
  public Python base retains its CPython and operating-system component notices;
  those components keep their own licenses. images/rtp and scripts/rtp-assets.sh
  provide the original worker sources, build recipes and locked base provenance.
  Offline worker rebuilds require that prepared base; the export does not claim
  a complete operating-system/compiler source distribution.

RTCP runtime image:
  The original SR+SDES compound parser, private observer and clients use MIT
  licensed project code with Python standard-library facilities. The pinned
  Python base retains its CPython/OS notices and licenses. No GStreamer or other
  media/session stack is bundled. Original sources, tests and build/base
  provenance accompany the worker export; rebuilding requires the prepared base.

DL/T645 runtime image:
  The finite software meter, stream framer and independent client use original
  MIT-licensed standard-library code adapted from authorized laboratory behavior.
  No third-party meter codec is a runtime dependency. The locked Python base
  retains CPython/OS notices. Worker sources, tests and build/base provenance
  accompany its export. The optional python-dlt645 codec used for a limited
  independent acceptance oracle keeps its own MIT license; it is not bundled
  into the manager or worker, nor represented as a complete native meter.

IEC101 runtime image and local helper:
  Official lib60870-C 2.4.1, fixed public commit
  7a388e3e133999e1ca77ba7521d55d074b7cd2bc, copyright Michael Zillgith and
  upstream contributors, is distributed under GPL-3.0-or-later. The project
  native C wrapper is also GPL-3.0-or-later. No native library patches are
  applied. The complete corresponding original source archive, full COPYING,
  wrapper, source SHA-256 and build recipes are in third-party/lib60870,
  workers/iec101 and images/iec101; the runtime includes them under
  /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab-iec101 and scripts/iec101-assets.sh exports
  them beside the runtime archive. Retain these corresponding sources and
  notices when redistributing the image/native binary; the MIT project license
  does not relicense this native component. The separate Rust/Web license index
  does not describe the worker's native dependency closure.
  The original Python bridge/master and local PTY helper use MIT and the Python
  standard library. The image preserves base CPython and OS notices. The deb
  installs the optional helper and depends on the host's Python3 package,
  retaining its upstream license through that package; Python is not linked
  into the Rust manager. No physical serial driver or proprietary stack is
  copied into the manager deb.

BFD runtime image:
  The autonomous single-hop state machine, six target-side scenarios, read-only
  observer and same-image client are original MIT-licensed project code using
  only the Python standard library. The locked base preserves CPython and OS
  notices. Runtime sources, tests and base/build provenance accompany the image
  export. Original FRR 8.4.4 is used separately as an independent native oracle;
  its executable, libraries, GPL source and component dependencies are not
  included in the standard-library worker or manager/deb distribution. Any
  separate redistribution of an oracle image retains its own complete
  corresponding-source and notice requirements; a config dry-run is not native
  interoperability evidence. Host iproute2 is supplied by its system package,
  invoked read-only by the manager and not copied into the repository/runtime.

This notice describes implemented HTTP, DNS, MQTT, Telnet, IEC104, MQTT-SN, RTP,
RTCP, DL/T645, IEC101 and BFD components. Other protocol
libraries and a complete offline distribution are not included or claimed here.

PROFINET runtime image:
  Native DCP responses come from official p-net 0.2.0, fixed upstream commit
  3e650720850e1f31f8a419c8aa135a2e81b44ddb, distributed here under its GPLv3
  option. The separate thin native application is GPL-3.0-only. OSAL commit
  88784fcfff7a1b74e20bdeb71a55c2cdd22b4a59 and cmake-tools commit
  45c751c60d75bbad5eb327624c259cc02b6564b2 retain their BSD-3-Clause licenses.
  Complete pinned native sources, full license texts, wrapper source, offline
  build recipes and compiler-input provenance accompany the exported assets
  and are included under /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab-profinet in the
  runtime image. Retain them when redistributing that native binary or image;
  the MIT project license does not relicense the native GPL component.
  The separate Python supervisor, disposable client and autonomous software
  PTCP implementation use the MIT project license and Python standard library.
  PTCP is not implemented by the p-net native stub and no native PTCP, cyclic
  PLC IO, IRT, physical clock precision or certification is claimed. Base
  CPython and OS components retain their own notices. The manager's Rust/Web
  dependency index is separate from this native worker's dependency closure.
